<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net">
  <diagram name="Visitor Pattern" id="visitor">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1434" dy="844" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1600">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        
        <!-- Title -->
        <mxCell id="title" value="VISITOR DESIGN PATTERN - Add Operations Without Changing Class Structure (Double Dispatch)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=20;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <mxCell id="problem_title" value="THE PROBLEM: Adding Operations Bloats Class &amp; Requires Retesting" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="70" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="problem" value="WITHOUT VISITOR PATTERN:&#xa;&#xa;class HotelRoom {&#xa;  int roomNumber;&#xa;  double basePrice;&#xa;  &#xa;  // Operation 1: Get price&#xa;  double getRoomPrice() { return basePrice * 1.2; }&#xa;  &#xa;  // Operation 2: Maintenance&#xa;  void initiateMaintenance() { /* maintenance logic */ }&#xa;  &#xa;  // Operation 3: Reserve room&#xa;  void reserveRoom() { /* reservation logic */ }&#xa;  &#xa;  // Operation 4: Calculate tax&#xa;  double calculateTax() { return basePrice * 0.18; }&#xa;  &#xa;  // Operation 5: Generate report&#xa;  String generateReport() { return 'Room: ' + roomNumber; }&#xa;  &#xa;  // ... 50 more operations added over time!&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;PROBLEMS:&#xa;&#xa;1. CLASS BLOAT:&#xa;   ✗ Initially 3 operations, tested &amp; working&#xa;   ✗ After 6 months → 50 operations in same class!&#xa;   ✗ Class becomes MASSIVE, hard to maintain&#xa;   ✗ Single Responsibility Principle violated&#xa;&#xa;2. RETESTING REQUIRED:&#xa;   ✗ Class fully tested with 3 operations&#xa;   ✗ Add Operation 4 (calculateTax) → MUST retest entire class!&#xa;   ✗ Add Operation 5 (generateReport) → MUST retest again!&#xa;   ✗ Every new operation risks breaking existing ones&#xa;   ✗ Regression testing nightmare&#xa;&#xa;3. OPERATIONS SCATTERED:&#xa;   ✗ Pricing logic in HotelRoom class&#xa;   ✗ Maintenance logic in HotelRoom class&#xa;   ✗ Reporting logic in HotelRoom class&#xa;   ✗ All mixed together — hard to find &amp; modify&#xa;&#xa;4. NO SEPARATION OF CONCERNS:&#xa;   ✗ HotelRoom class knows about:&#xa;     - Room data (number, price)&#xa;     - Pricing logic&#xa;     - Maintenance logic&#xa;     - Reservation logic&#xa;     - Tax calculation&#xa;     - Report generation&#xa;   ✗ Too many responsibilities!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WHAT IF:&#xa;  - Need to add 10 more operations?&#xa;  - Each operation complex (100+ lines)?&#xa;  - Different teams work on different operations?&#xa;  - Operations change frequently but room structure stable?&#xa;&#xa;  CLASS BECOMES UNMAINTAINABLE!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="110" width="1520" height="720" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- SOLUTION -->
        <mxCell id="solution_title" value="THE SOLUTION: Visitor Pattern - Separate Operations from Object Structure" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="870" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="solution" value="VISITOR PATTERN SOLUTION:&#xa;&#xa;// ELEMENT (objects on which operations performed)&#xa;interface RoomElement {&#xa;  void accept(RoomVisitor visitor);  // ONLY method!&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class SingleRoom implements RoomElement {&#xa;  int roomNumber;&#xa;  double basePrice;&#xa;  &#xa;  // NO operations here! Only accept()&#xa;  public void accept(RoomVisitor visitor) {&#xa;    visitor.visit(this);  // Double dispatch!&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class DoubleRoom implements RoomElement {&#xa;  public void accept(RoomVisitor visitor) {&#xa;    visitor.visit(this);&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class DeluxeRoom implements RoomElement {&#xa;  public void accept(RoomVisitor visitor) {&#xa;    visitor.visit(this);&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// VISITOR (operations extracted into separate classes!)&#xa;interface RoomVisitor {&#xa;  void visit(SingleRoom room);  // Operation for SingleRoom&#xa;  void visit(DoubleRoom room);  // Operation for DoubleRoom&#xa;  void visit(DeluxeRoom room);  // Operation for DeluxeRoom&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;// OPERATION 1: Pricing (in OWN class!)&#xa;class RoomPricingVisitor implements RoomVisitor {&#xa;  public void visit(SingleRoom room) {&#xa;    System.out.println('SingleRoom price: ' + room.basePrice * 1.0);&#xa;  }&#xa;  public void visit(DoubleRoom room) {&#xa;    System.out.println('DoubleRoom price: ' + room.basePrice * 1.5);&#xa;  }&#xa;  public void visit(DeluxeRoom room) {&#xa;    System.out.println('DeluxeRoom price: ' + room.basePrice * 2.0);&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;// OPERATION 2: Maintenance (in OWN class!)&#xa;class RoomMaintenanceVisitor implements RoomVisitor {&#xa;  public void visit(SingleRoom room) {&#xa;    System.out.println('Maintenance for SingleRoom: Basic cleaning');&#xa;  }&#xa;  public void visit(DoubleRoom room) {&#xa;    System.out.println('Maintenance for DoubleRoom: Deep cleaning');&#xa;  }&#xa;  public void visit(DeluxeRoom room) {&#xa;    System.out.println('Maintenance for DeluxeRoom: Premium service');&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;// OPERATION 3: Reserve Room (in OWN class!)&#xa;class RoomReservationVisitor implements RoomVisitor {&#xa;  public void visit(SingleRoom room) { /* reservation logic */ }&#xa;  public void visit(DoubleRoom room) { /* reservation logic */ }&#xa;  public void visit(DeluxeRoom room) { /* reservation logic */ }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;// Want NEW operation? Just add NEW visitor! (no changes to room classes!)&#xa;class TaxCalculationVisitor implements RoomVisitor {&#xa;  public void visit(SingleRoom room) { /* tax logic */ }&#xa;  public void visit(DoubleRoom room) { /* tax logic */ }&#xa;  public void visit(DeluxeRoom room) { /* tax logic */ }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// CLIENT&#xa;class Client {&#xa;  public static void main(String[] args) {&#xa;    RoomElement singleRoom = new SingleRoom();&#xa;    &#xa;    // Want pricing? Use pricing visitor&#xa;    singleRoom.accept(new RoomPricingVisitor());&#xa;    &#xa;    // Want maintenance? Use maintenance visitor&#xa;    singleRoom.accept(new RoomMaintenanceVisitor());&#xa;    &#xa;    // Want reservation? Use reservation visitor&#xa;    singleRoom.accept(new RoomReservationVisitor());&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;BENEFITS:&#xa;  ✓ SingleRoom class NEVER changes (tested once, done!)&#xa;  ✓ Add new operation → Create new Visitor (no retesting rooms!)&#xa;  ✓ Operations separated (1 visitor = 1 operation)&#xa;  ✓ Easy to find: Pricing logic in RoomPricingVisitor&#xa;  ✓ Easy to test: Test RoomPricingVisitor independently&#xa;  ✓ Horizontal growth (add visitors), not vertical (bloat class)&#xa;  ✓ Open-Closed Principle (open for extension, closed for modification)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY INSIGHT:&#xa;  Operations move FROM room classes TO visitor classes&#xa;  Room classes: Stable, minimal, tested once&#xa;  Visitor classes: Grow independently, easy to add/modify" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="910" width="1520" height="1080" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- DOUBLE DISPATCH -->
        <mxCell id="dispatch_title" value="DOUBLE DISPATCH EXPLAINED" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2030" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="dispatch" value="SINGLE DISPATCH (normal polymorphism):&#xa;  RoomElement room = new SingleRoom();  // Reference type: RoomElement&#xa;  room.getPrice();  // Which getPrice()? Depends on OBJECT type (SingleRoom)&#xa;  &#xa;  Method determined by: CALLER object type (runtime type)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;DOUBLE DISPATCH (Visitor Pattern):&#xa;  RoomElement room = new SingleRoom();&#xa;  RoomVisitor visitor = new RoomPricingVisitor();&#xa;  &#xa;  room.accept(visitor);  // FIRST dispatch: Which accept()? → SingleRoom.accept()&#xa;    ↓&#xa;  visitor.visit(this);   // SECOND dispatch: Which visit()? → RoomPricingVisitor.visit(SingleRoom)&#xa;  &#xa;  Method determined by: CALLER object + ARGUMENT object&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WHY DOUBLE DISPATCH?&#xa;&#xa;STEP 1: room.accept(visitor)&#xa;  Caller: room (RoomElement reference, SingleRoom object)&#xa;  FIRST DISPATCH: JVM checks runtime type → SingleRoom&#xa;  Calls: SingleRoom.accept(visitor)&#xa;&#xa;STEP 2: visitor.visit(this)  (inside SingleRoom.accept)&#xa;  Caller: visitor (RoomVisitor reference, RoomPricingVisitor object)&#xa;  Argument: this (SingleRoom object)&#xa;  &#xa;  SECOND DISPATCH: JVM checks:&#xa;    - visitor runtime type → RoomPricingVisitor&#xa;    - argument type → SingleRoom&#xa;  &#xa;  Calls: RoomPricingVisitor.visit(SingleRoom)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;RESULT:&#xa;  Correct method invoked based on:&#xa;    1. Which room (SingleRoom, DoubleRoom, DeluxeRoom)&#xa;    2. Which operation (Pricing, Maintenance, Reservation)&#xa;  &#xa;  Both factors determine method call!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;WITHOUT DOUBLE DISPATCH (impossible!):&#xa;  RoomVisitor visitor = new RoomPricingVisitor();&#xa;  RoomElement room = new SingleRoom();&#xa;  &#xa;  visitor.visit(room);  // ✗ Compile error! visit(RoomElement) doesn't exist!&#xa;                        // Only visit(SingleRoom), visit(DoubleRoom) exist&#xa;  &#xa;  Need: visitor knows exact type (SingleRoom), not RoomElement&#xa;  Solution: room.accept(visitor) → passes 'this' (exact type)!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2070" width="1520" height="640" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <mxCell id="q_title" value="INTERVIEW QUESTIONS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2750" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="questions" value="Q1: Visitor vs Strategy Pattern — both extract operations?&#xa;A1: INTENT differs! VISITOR: Add NEW operations to EXISTING class hierarchy without modifying classes. For multiple elements (SingleRoom, DoubleRoom). STRATEGY: Swap ALGORITHM for single object at runtime. For behavior change. Example: Visitor for room operations (pricing, maintenance on all room types). Strategy for sorting algorithm (QuickSort, MergeSort swap). Visitor = operations on hierarchy, Strategy = algorithm swap.&#xa;&#xa;Q2: When NOT to use Visitor Pattern?&#xa;A2: DON'T use when: (1) Element hierarchy changes frequently (adding new room type requires changing ALL visitors — painful!), (2) Few operations, many element types (visitor overkill), (3) Operations rarely added (no benefit from separation). USE when: Operations added frequently, element structure stable, operations complex (worth separating). Trade-off: stable elements + growing operations = Visitor shines!&#xa;&#xa;Q3: Visitor Pattern violates encapsulation?&#xa;A3: YES, potential issue! Visitor needs access to Element internals (room.basePrice). Solutions: (1) Provide public getters (visitor calls room.getBasePrice()), (2) Make visitor FRIEND class (language-dependent), (3) Accept trade-off (flexibility > strict encapsulation). Alternative: Pass only PUBLIC data to visitor. Visitor works on public interface, not private fields. Balance: encapsulation vs extensibility.&#xa;&#xa;Q4: How to handle new Element type in Visitor Pattern?&#xa;A4: PROBLEM: Add PresidentialSuite → must modify ALL visitors (RoomPricingVisitor, RoomMaintenanceVisitor, etc.) to add visit(PresidentialSuite). PAINFUL! SOLUTION: (1) Default visitor method (visit(RoomElement) base case), (2) Acyclic Visitor Pattern (separate interface per element), (3) Accept that element hierarchy should be STABLE. Visitor best when: operations grow (easy), elements stable (rare).&#xa;&#xa;Q5: Real-world Visitor Pattern examples?&#xa;A5: (1) Compilers: AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) nodes stable, operations vary (type-checking, code generation, optimization). Each visitor = one compiler pass. (2) File systems: FileSystemElement (File, Directory) stable. Visitors: SizeCalculator, SearchVisitor, PermissionChecker. (3) XML/DOM parsers: Node types stable (Element, Attribute, Text). Visitors: XPathEvaluator, XMLValidator, XMLSerializer. Anywhere: stable structure + growing operations!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2800" width="1520" height="480" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
